        ["After 12 weeks of endurance training, a client can run at a faster pace before lactate climbs sharply. What adaptation best explains this?",["A higher lactate threshold from more mitochondria and better lactate clearance","A lower maximum heart rate that slows how fast lactate is produced","Fewer capillaries in trained muscle so less lactate reaches the blood","A shift toward more type IIx fibers that produce less lactate overall"],"Endurance training increases mitochondria and capillaries and improves lactate clearance, raising the threshold. Maximum heart rate is mostly unchanged by training."],
        ["During the first minute of a moderate cycling bout, oxygen uptake has not yet reached steady state. What covers the energy gap?",["The oxygen deficit, met by the phosphagen and glycolytic systems","EPOC, met by oxygen stored in the lungs and blood vessels","Fat oxidation, which rises immediately to meet full demand","Protein breakdown, which supplies ATP during the first minute"],"Oxygen uptake lags at exercise onset, and the shortfall (oxygen deficit) is covered anaerobically. EPOC happens after exercise, not at the start."],
        ["A client believes lactate causes the muscle soreness they feel 2 days after a hard workout. What is the BEST explanation to give?",["Lactate clears within about an hour; delayed soreness reflects muscle microdamage","Lactate stays in the muscle for 2 days and causes the soreness","Soreness comes from phosphocreatine depletion lasting several days","Soreness means the oxidative system has stopped working properly"],"Blood lactate returns to baseline within roughly an hour after exercise. Delayed onset muscle soreness is linked to eccentric-induced microdamage and inflammation."],
        ["A patient with chronic heart failure tolerates only short bouts of walking. Which session design BEST matches their limited aerobic capacity?",["Short walking intervals with rest breaks, progressing work time","Continuous walking for 45 minutes at the highest tolerable speed","Maximal 10-second sprints with long rests to train the PCr system","Heavy resistance training only, with no aerobic component at all"],"Interval walking lets the patient accumulate aerobic work within their tolerance, and work time is progressed as capacity improves. Continuous long bouts or maximal sprints exceed their current capacity."],
        ["Roughly how long does near-complete phosphocreatine resynthesis take after a maximal effort?",["A few minutes","About 24 hours","About 5 seconds","About 30 minutes"],"Most PCr is restored within a few minutes, which is why power sets use long rests. Thirty minutes and 24 hours are far too long; 5 seconds is far too short."],
        ["Why does carbohydrate become the dominant fuel as exercise intensity rises?",["It produces ATP faster and with less oxygen per ATP than fat","It is the only fuel that the mitochondria are able to use","Fat cannot be oxidized when heart rate is above 100 bpm","It is stored in larger amounts in the body than fat"],"Carbohydrate yields ATP more rapidly and needs less oxygen per ATP, which matters as demand rises. Fat stores are much larger, and fat is still oxidized at higher heart rates."]
