        ["A patient taking a beta-blocker is starting an aerobic program. How should intensity be monitored?",["Use RPE because heart rate response is blunted","Use 220 - age and 85% of that value","Use the Karvonen formula unchanged","Avoid all monitoring since beta-blockers make exercise safe"],"Beta-blockers lower resting and exercise heart rate, so standard HR targets would be wrong. RPE gives a reliable measure of effort."],
        ["After 6 months of endurance training, which change in resting physiology is expected?",["Lower resting heart rate with higher stroke volume","Higher resting heart rate with lower stroke volume","Higher resting heart rate with no change in stroke volume","No change in heart rate or stroke volume"],"Training enlarges the heart's pumping capacity, so stroke volume rises and the same output is met at a lower heart rate."],
        ["A patient holds their breath and strains while lifting a heavy box during a session. Why should you cue them to exhale during the lift?",["Breath holding with straining (Valsalva) sharply raises blood pressure","Exhaling during the effort increases muscle force by about half","Holding the breath lowers the heart rate to a dangerously slow level","Exhaling during the lift stops lactate from being produced"],"The Valsalva maneuver raises pressure in the chest and abdomen and spikes blood pressure. This is a risk for people with hypertension or heart disease."],
        ["What is the Tanaka formula for age-predicted maximum heart rate?",["208 - (0.7 x age)","220 - (0.7 x age)","206 - (0.5 x age)","210 - (0.8 x age)"],"Tanaka's equation is 208 - 0.7 x age and is thought to be more accurate in older adults than 220 - age. Both are estimates."],
        ["A patient walks on a treadmill at an intensity of 3 METs. About what oxygen uptake does this represent?",["About 10.5 mL/kg/min","About 3.0 mL/kg/min","About 35 mL/kg/min","About 1.2 mL/kg/min"],"One MET is conventionally 3.5 mL/kg/min, so 3 METs is 3 x 3.5 = 10.5 mL/kg/min. Answering 3.0 confuses METs with oxygen uptake."],
        ["Endurance training increases a client's VO2max. Using the Fick equation, which changes BEST explain this?",["Higher maximal stroke volume and a wider a-vO2 difference","Higher maximal heart rate and a narrower a-vO2 difference","Lower stroke volume with a much higher maximal heart rate","No change in cardiac output, only higher breathing rate"],"VO2max = Q x a-vO2 difference. Training raises maximal stroke volume (so maximal Q) and improves muscle extraction, while maximal heart rate changes little."],
        ["During a 60-minute steady run on a hot day, a runner's heart rate rises from 145 to 160 bpm at the same pace. What is the MOST likely explanation?",["Cardiovascular drift from fluid loss and falling stroke volume","A sudden drop in VO2max caused by the hot environment","Normal training adaptation that lowers stroke volume over time","Lactate threshold falling to zero after 30 minutes of running"],"Dehydration and skin blood flow reduce stroke volume during prolonged exercise, so heart rate rises to keep cardiac output steady. This is cardiovascular drift."],
        ["A 30-year-old has a resting heart rate of 60 bpm. Using 220 - age and the Karvonen method, what is the target heart rate at 70% intensity?",["151 bpm","133 bpm","163 bpm","142 bpm"],"HRmax = 190, HRR = 130, 0.7 x 130 = 91, and 91 + 60 = 151 bpm. Taking 70% of HRmax alone gives 133 bpm, which ignores resting heart rate."],
        ["A patient with atrial fibrillation is starting an aerobic program. Which method is MOST appropriate for monitoring intensity?",["RPE and the talk test, since heart rate is irregular and unreliable","Karvonen target heart rate calculated from 220 minus age","A fixed target of 85% of the age-predicted maximum heart rate","Pulse taken for 6 seconds and multiplied by 10 at each stage"],"In atrial fibrillation, heart rate is irregular and does not track intensity well, so perceived exertion and the talk test are preferred. A short 6-second count is especially inaccurate with an irregular rhythm."]
